You bought a car. You expected it to run. Instead, it keeps breaking down.

At first, you thought it was bad luck. Then came the second trip to the repair shop. Then the third. Now you’re asking the same question many California drivers face: Is my car a lemon?

A lemon car is one that has a significant defect the manufacturer can’t fix within a reasonable number of attempts. It might be a transmission issue, a recurring brake failure, or something that puts your safety at risk. If the problem affects how the car drives, what it’s worth, or how safe it is, you may have a case.

[Less than half a percent](https://publicinterestnetwork.org//srv/htdocs/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Auto-Lemon-Index-CAP-CARS-FG-May22-1.pdf) of new vehicles sold in California are considered lemons. That number might feel small, but California registers over 2 million new cars each year. With a population of nearly 39 million people, that still adds up to thousands of lemon cars on the road. Thousands.

This guide will help you find out. We’ll walk through [how the California lemon law works](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law), how to document your repair history, and what steps to take if your car qualifies. Whether you’re already frustrated or just starting to suspect a problem, you’ll get answers.

Let’s start with the basics.

## What Is the California Lemon Law?

The California lemon law, officially [California Civil Code Section 1793.22](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&amp&division=3.&amp&title=1.7.&amp&part=4.&amp&chapter=1.&amp&article=3.), protects consumers who purchase or lease defective vehicles that the manufacturer cannot repair within a reasonable number of attempts.

It applies to both personal and certain small business vehicles, giving buyers legal options when a car turns out to be more trouble than transportation.

## Who Does the Law Protect?

This law covers individuals who purchase or lease vehicles primarily for personal, family, or household use. It also extends to small businesses with five or fewer registered vehicles in California, as long as each weighs under 10,000 pounds.

Whether you bought your car outright or signed a lease, you still have rights under the California lemon law if the vehicle meets the required criteria.

## What Types of Vehicles Are Covered?

Most people associate the law with brand-new cars, but it can apply to used and leased vehicles too, if they’re still under the original manufacturer’s warranty.

The lemon law covers sedans, trucks, SUVs, vans, and even dealer-owned or demonstrator vehicles. For motor homes, it applies to the chassis and drivetrain components, but not to the living quarters.

Vehicles sold “as-is” with no remaining manufacturer warranty typically don’t qualify under this law. In those cases, other consumer protections may be more appropriate.

What Qualifies a Vehicle as a “Lemon” in California?

## What Does the Lemon Law Offer in Return?

If your lemon car meets the law’s requirements, you may be entitled to a replacement vehicle or a refund. The refund includes not just the purchase price but also incidental costs like sales tax, registration fees, rental cars, and towing charges.

The law also allows for attorney fees to be paid by the manufacturer, which means many people can get legal help without paying out of pocket.

## How Does a Car Qualify as a Lemon in California?

Simply having a defect does not automatically mean the vehicle is a lemon. Each state has unique requirements that need to be met. In California, a vehicle may be deemed a lemon if:

- The defect was officially reported to the manufacturer within the terms of the warranty (either the original manufacturer’s warranty or dealer’s warranty for used cars);

- The defect was not a result of driver abuse or neglect;

- The defect substantially impairs the vehicle’s safety, functionality, or value;

- A manufacturer-certified repair facility has been given a reasonable number of attempts to fix the defect (usually at least two);or

- The vehicle has been out of service for repairs for 30 or more cumulative days.

If you can prove your defective vehicle meets these requirements with documentation, you may be eligible for a lemon law claim in California.

## What Counts as a “Defect” Under the California Lemon Law?

To qualify as a lemon car, your vehicle must have a defect that the manufacturer can’t fix within a reasonable amount of time.

But not all car problems meet that threshold. The law focuses on issues that interfere with how the car works, how safe it is, or how much it’s worth.

## Defects That Affect Safety, Use, or Value

Common qualifying defects include:

- Engine stalling, overheating, or misfiring

- Transmission slipping or failing to shift properly

- Electrical failures that affect starting, lights, or steering

- Brake problems or anti-lock brake system (ABS) malfunctions

- Faulty airbags, backup cameras, or other safety tech

These issues make it difficult, or even dangerous, to rely on your vehicle. Whether the problem is mechanical, electrical, or software-related, what matters is how it affects your ability to drive the car safely and predictably.

## What “Substantial Impairment” Means

The legal standard isn’t perfection; it’s practicality. A defect creates a “substantial impairment” if it reduces your ability to use the car as intended, puts you at risk, or significantly lowers the vehicle’s market value.

That threshold can vary based on the type of vehicle and the nature of the problem.

A broken radio may not be enough to qualify. But a recurring fuel system leak, for example, almost certainly does. If the issue forces you to change how you drive, how often you drive, or whether you feel safe behind the wheel, it likely meets the legal definition of a defect.

## What Is a Reasonable Number of Repair Attempts?

If you’re asking, “Is my car a lemon?” the number of repair attempts is one of the first things to review. Under the California lemon law, manufacturers must be given a fair shot to fix the defect.

But they don’t get unlimited chances.

## What the Law Presumes Is “Reasonable”

California law sets a clear guideline. If any of the following occur within the warranty period, your vehicle is presumed to be a lemon:

- The manufacturer or dealer tried to fix the same defect four or more times, or

- The defect poses a risk of death or serious injury and wasn’t fixed after two attempts, or

- The car has been in the shop for more than 30 days total for warranty-related repairs

These aren’t hard limits. Your car may still qualify even if it falls outside this window, but these milestones carry legal weight and make your case stronger.

## Why Documentation Matters

Lemon law cases often hinge on paperwork. Keep every repair order and service invoice. Each document should show the date the car was dropped off, the issue reported, what work was performed, and when it was returned. The more complete your records, the easier it is to prove how many times the defect was addressed and how long the vehicle was out of service.

If you notice the same issue showing up on multiple repair orders, highlight that pattern. Grouping similar complaints across visits helps paint a clear picture of an unresolved problem.

## Quick Checklist: Do You Meet the Repair Threshold?

Ask yourself:

- Have I brought the car in at least 2 to 4 times for the same issue?

- Has it been in the shop for 30 or more days total?

- Is the defect something that affects safety or drivability?

If the answer is yes to any of these, your next question should be: Is my car a lemon under California law? And you may be closer to a yes than you think.

## How to Determine If Your Car Is a Lemon: A Step-by-Step Process

If you’ve been wondering, “Is my car a lemon?” the answer starts with your service history. Even if the problem feels obvious, you’ll need a clear paper trail to back it up.

Here’s how to evaluate your case the way a lemon law lawyer in California would.

## Step 1: Gather Your Repair Records

Collect every repair order or service invoice from the dealership or certified repair center. These documents are the backbone of your case. If you’re missing any, contact the service department and request copies.

## Step 2: Sort Records in Chronological Order

Organize your records by date. This helps you track the timeline of when the issue first appeared and how long it took to get resolved or whether it was resolved at all.

## Step 3: Summarize the Complaints

For each visit, jot down the problem you reported. Look for consistency. Did the same issue come up over and over again? Was the problem described the same way, or did it evolve over time?

## Step 4: Group Recurring Issues

If you reported similar problems across multiple visits, group them together. For example, if your transmission slipped on three different visits, that’s a pattern. Patterns make cases stronger.

## Step 5: Count Days Out of Service

Tally up the number of days your vehicle was in the shop. Even if the visits were short, they add up. If your car was out of service for 30 days or more, that may qualify under the California lemon law, even if no single repair visit was long.

## Step 6: Compare to the Legal Thresholds

Now that you have the full picture, ask:

- Did the issue appear four or more times?

- Was it never fixed after two attempts, even though it was safety-related?

- Did repairs take 30 days or more in total?

If you can say yes to any of those, you may be eligible for a lemon law buyback. [Here’s what that process looks like.](/blog/california-buyback-lemon-law-what-happens-to-lemon-vehicles)

## Does the Lemon Law Cover Used Cars in California?

Many drivers think the California lemon law only protects new vehicles. That’s not true. In many cases, used or Certified Pre-Owned (CPO) vehicles also qualify, especially if they’re still under a dealer warranty.

To be eligible, the used vehicle must have been sold with a written warranty from the dealer, which is displayed on the vehicle’s buyer’s guide. The law does not protect vehicles sold “as-is,” which is why it’s important to read the sales agreement carefully.

Certified Pre-Owned vehicles are a common source of confusion. These cars often come with extended warranties or a dealer warranty. That warranty coverage can make them eligible for protection under California’s lemon law, just like a brand-new car.

## What If the Manufacturer Denies Your Claim?

It happens more often than you’d expect. You bring your car in multiple times. The dealer can’t fix the issue. You ask about your rights, and the manufacturer says your vehicle doesn’t qualify or they ignore you altogether.

Don’t stop there.

Manufacturers often push back to avoid the cost of a buyback or replacement. They may question whether the defect is real or claim it doesn’t meet the legal standard. That’s where legal support can make a difference.

A skilled lemon law lawyer in California knows how to escalate the situation. They can file a formal demand, gather expert opinions, and bring the case to arbitration or court if needed. With the right attorney, most valid claims settle before a lawsuit ever becomes necessary.

If your car meets the legal thresholds and your claim was denied, you still have options. Don’t assume the answer is final - especially when the law is on your side.

